<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mask of Agamemnon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">16th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century-bce">16th century BCE</a> · Also known as Death Mask of Agamemnon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mask of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agamemnon">Agamemnon</a> is a gold funerary mask found by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-schliemann">Heinrich Schliemann</a> in 1876 in Grave V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grave-circle-a">Grave Circle A</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a>, now in the National Archaeological Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. Hammered from a single thick sheet of gold, it shows a face with a pointed beard and moustache. Schliemann named it after the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a>, but it dates to around the 16th century BCE, centuries before the mythical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a>. Its authenticity has been questioned by William Calder III and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Traill, but modern research suggests it is genuine.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mask-of-agamemnon.jpg" alt="Mask of Agamemnon" width="320" height="303" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gleb Simonov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athens_%E2%80%94_Mask_of_Agamemnon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-schliemann">Heinrich Schliemann</a> found the gold funerary mask in 1876 in a shaft tomb designated Grave V in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grave-circle-a">Grave Circle A</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a>. Eight men were found in Grave Circle A, all with weapons in their graves, but only five had masks, and those lay in Grave IV and Grave V. The quantities of gold and the carefully worked objects indicate honour, wealth and status. The historian Cathy Gere has called the mask the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mona-lisa">Mona Lisa</a> of prehistory.</p><p>Schliemann named it after the mythical Greek king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agamemnon">Agamemnon</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s Iliad and took it as evidence that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a> was a real event. After finding it he notified King George I, and is supposed to have telegraphed that he had gazed upon the face of Agamemnon. Near the end of his life he accepted doubts about the mask's owner, and is quoted as saying: &quot;So this is not Agamemnon... these are not his ornaments? All right, let's call him Schulze.&quot; Modern research dates the mask to around the 16th century BCE, 300 to 400 years before the period of the mythical Trojan War.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The mask was made from a single thick sheet of gold, heated and hammered against a wooden background, with the details chased on later with a sharp tool. It is displayed in the National Archaeological Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. It differs from three of the other masks found: it is three-dimensional rather than flat, the ears are cut out, the eyes are shown both open and shut, the mouth is well defined and the brows form two arches. Alone among depictions of faces in Mycenaean art it has a full pointed beard with a handlebar moustache.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In the latter half of the 20th century and the early 21st, the mask's authenticity was formally questioned, chiefly by William Calder III and David Traill, and Archaeology magazine ran articles presenting both sides. Those who argued fraud pointed to Schliemann's reputation for salting digs with objects from elsewhere and held that he could have had the mask made on the model of the other Mycenaean masks.</p><p>Defenders replied that after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> controversy the Greek Archaeological Society had sent Panagiotis Stamatakis to supervise the excavation. The dig was closed on 26 and 27 November for a Sunday holiday and rain, and Stamatakis arranged credible witnesses before it reopened. The three other masks were found on the 28th and the Mask of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agamemnon">Agamemnon</a> on the 30th. They also argued that the lip, the triangular beard and the beard's detail closely resemble the mane of the gold lion-head rhyton from Shaft Grave IV, and that Schliemann's duplicity has been exaggerated, claiming that his attackers conducted a vendetta. Modern archaeological research suggests the mask is genuine.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mask of Agamemnon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
